It's almost 30,000 feet high to the summit, to the tippy top point.

01:28:26.627 --> 01:28:27.627
Tallest mountain in the world.

01:28:27.767 --> 01:28:34.031
But also the Himalayas hold over 50 mountains that exceed 23,000 feet.

01:28:34.431 --> 01:28:35.111
Yeah.

01:28:35.131 --> 01:28:41.174
So that's 7,000 meters for those and over almost 9,000 meters for Everest.

01:28:41.695 --> 01:28:45.016
Now for the sticklers out there, these are the highest mountains in the world.

01:28:45.345 --> 01:28:47.006
We are aware of Hawaii.

01:28:47.346 --> 01:28:49.267
These are not the tallest mountains.

01:28:49.347 --> 01:28:50.547
Yes, exactly.

01:28:50.707 --> 01:28:51.248
They're base.

01:28:51.468 --> 01:28:51.628
Yes.

01:28:51.648 --> 01:28:53.329
These are the highest above sea level.

01:28:53.349 --> 01:28:53.829
Yeah, yeah.

01:28:54.109 --> 01:28:57.310
Not including the ones in the ocean, terrestrial mountains.

01:28:59.931 --> 01:29:05.694
Now these mountains, of course, began to form about 50 million years ago as the collision

01:29:05.734 --> 01:29:06.094
started.

01:29:06.114 --> 01:29:06.954
And they're still at it.

01:29:07.134 --> 01:29:07.835
And they're still at it.

01:29:08.115 --> 01:29:09.655
India is still moving north.

01:29:10.242 --> 01:29:13.284
So it is still affecting the height of these mountains.

01:29:13.804 --> 01:29:18.267
Now the reason they're not constantly just getting taller is erosion and gravity.

01:29:18.627 --> 01:29:18.867
Yes.

01:29:19.647 --> 01:29:23.970
So mountains are wearing away with rain and wind and weather and all that good stuff.

01:29:24.270 --> 01:29:28.432
But also mountains are heavy and will actually push the land underneath them down.

01:29:28.612 --> 01:29:28.792
Yes.

01:29:29.323 --> 01:29:38.548
I have seen discussions about what is the limit of how big a mountain can be on Earth

01:29:38.608 --> 01:29:40.629
before it just starts collapsing.

01:29:41.089 --> 01:29:45.811
And I have seen some, and I don't have any scientific references for this, but I have

01:29:45.831 --> 01:29:52.995
seen some people suggest that the Himalayas might be near the limit of how tall a mountain

01:29:53.095 --> 01:29:54.055
can be on Earth.

01:29:54.505 --> 01:29:56.186
Which that makes sense to me.

01:29:56.606 --> 01:30:02.089
Now when we say growth and changing height, we're talking like a centimeter a year growing

01:30:02.189 --> 01:30:02.589
upward.

01:30:03.029 --> 01:30:05.410
So it's not shooting up.

01:30:05.430 --> 01:30:07.151
They're not going through mountain puberty anymore.

01:30:07.751 --> 01:30:09.572
It is constantly in fluctuation.

01:30:09.592 --> 01:30:11.773
These are an active mountain range.

01:30:12.034 --> 01:30:13.934
They are not old and tired.

01:30:14.343 --> 01:30:19.485
But the story of this collision is not as clear cut as it may seem.

01:30:20.226 --> 01:30:23.227
What I have learned is that mountain building events are never simple.

01:30:23.307 --> 01:30:23.587
Right?

01:30:24.287 --> 01:30:30.290
When we first see the evidence of collision, of the Himalayas raising and of India hitting

01:30:30.390 --> 01:30:33.892
Asia is at earliest 55 million years ago.

01:30:33.912 --> 01:30:35.793
That's the first signs.

01:30:36.304 --> 01:30:43.827
But according to our records of what we found of where India would be at the time, I found

01:30:43.927 --> 01:30:49.029
one that's put it strikingly far south to be hitting Asia.

01:30:50.210 --> 01:30:58.193
And this has revealed that if India is indeed colliding with Asia at that time where it

01:30:58.267 --> 01:31:08.091
was, there is a missing length of land about 2,500 kilometers long that should be there

01:31:08.451 --> 01:31:10.052
but is not on India today.

01:31:10.952 --> 01:31:15.934
So for it to be hitting India when it did, it should have been much bigger than it is

01:31:15.974 --> 01:31:16.235
today.

01:31:16.795 --> 01:31:18.055
So maybe it got smushed.

01:31:18.495 --> 01:31:21.317
There's a few different ideas of what might have happened.

01:31:21.563 --> 01:31:23.104
This is where it gets complicated.

01:31:23.964 --> 01:31:26.786
So one thing that might have happened is it could have been subducted.

01:31:27.106 --> 01:31:32.628
That a chunk of India, the greater India as it's often called, when it was bigger, when

01:31:32.688 --> 01:31:38.391
it hit Asia, a portion of it, not all of it because obviously it's not still subducting,

01:31:38.771 --> 01:31:45.134
but the first chunk went under Asia and was eaten and disappeared.

01:31:45.506 --> 01:31:49.888
So we lost that chunk, making India seem much smaller than it would have been.

01:31:50.628 --> 01:31:54.190
Now the issue with this is why did you stop subducting?

01:31:55.130 --> 01:31:57.311
If you subducted that part, why didn't the rest subduct?

01:31:57.811 --> 01:32:02.293
The fact that the mountains are still going up seems to suggest that maybe that's not

01:32:03.093 --> 01:32:10.396
a little bit of evidence against if you're now hitting head on, so maybe some subduction

01:32:10.426 --> 01:32:12.426
but it doesn't fully satisfy everyone.

01:32:13.027 --> 01:32:18.008
Another idea is not that India was actually bigger, but that instead it was relatively

01:32:18.088 --> 01:32:24.051
similar size to what we see now, but as it moved it pushed land out of the way that would

01:32:24.091 --> 01:32:27.712
have been closer than it seems now.

01:32:27.892 --> 01:32:34.195
So that the face of Asia that it was coming to meet was low, extended farther south than

01:32:34.235 --> 01:32:34.855
we realize.

01:32:34.945 --> 01:32:40.868
Yes, and then India smushed it aside, pushed it all up and out of the way as it moved in.

01:32:41.048 --> 01:32:45.711
And so now where the barrier is, it seems like Asia is too far up.

01:32:46.451 --> 01:32:46.951
Gotcha.

01:32:46.971 --> 01:32:49.272
So maybe it just moved stuff.

01:32:49.793 --> 01:32:55.656
Then I came across some research from 2013, which is super interesting, which has found

01:32:55.716 --> 01:32:59.038
evidence that the collision actually might have happened in two stages.

01:32:59.545 --> 01:32:59.945
Of course.

01:33:00.306 --> 01:33:00.826
Mountain building.

01:33:00.886 --> 01:33:01.086
Yep.

01:33:02.006 --> 01:33:08.169
At 50 million and 40 million years ago, they came to this conclusion by, to try to understand

01:33:08.749 --> 01:33:14.892
the collision of India, looking at a similar collision that is currently going on between

01:33:15.012 --> 01:33:20.015
Australia and a series of islands called the Sunda Arc.

01:33:20.386 --> 01:33:24.607
Now this has only been happening over the last 2 million years, so it's more recent,

01:33:24.627 --> 01:33:29.349
it's not nearly as dramatic, but we are seeing a similar impact.

01:33:29.889 --> 01:33:36.252
They looked for isotopes that were known to morph due to things like time and tectonic

01:33:36.792 --> 01:33:37.312
disturbance.

01:33:37.873 --> 01:33:42.815
They found two groups of isotopes, one which is an isotope of lutetium that decays into

01:33:42.824 --> 01:33:47.426
hafnium, and another which is samarium and decays into neodymium.

01:33:47.846 --> 01:33:53.889
They found in the Australia example that rocks that were high in these isotopes likely formed

01:33:54.149 --> 01:33:59.731
before the collision, and rocks that were low in these isotopes likely formed after

01:34:00.171 --> 01:34:00.612
the collision.

01:34:01.092 --> 01:34:06.334
And when they went to the Himalayas, to look at a section known as the Koestan Ladakh Arc,

01:34:06.844 --> 01:34:11.627
this arc is found in the northwestern edge of the Himalayas, they found similar signatures

01:34:11.667 --> 01:34:17.930
in the rocks there, with the rocks older than 50 million years pre-collision, same ratios

01:34:17.970 --> 01:34:26.934
of isotopes north and south of the arc, with rocks younger than 50 million years along

01:34:26.966 --> 01:34:32.748
the southern edge of the arc, rocks found younger than 50 million years on the southern

01:34:32.788 --> 01:34:37.390
edge of that arc showed a range of ratios in the isotopes, suggesting that there had

01:34:37.410 --> 01:34:44.092
been tectonic disturbance, but along the northern edge of the arc, we didn't see that same disturbance

01:34:44.172 --> 01:34:45.833
until 40 million years ago.

01:34:46.813 --> 01:34:50.054
So maybe two pulses of tectonic activity.

01:34:50.204 --> 01:34:55.586
And what they think those pulses were is that at about 50 million years ago, India hit a

01:34:55.706 --> 01:35:03.930
string of islands in between itself and Asia, which have been dubbed the Koestan Ladakh

01:35:04.450 --> 01:35:09.032
Arc, and then it smushed those islands into Asia about 40 million years ago.

01:35:10.173 --> 01:35:12.614
That would make up that gap in distance as well.

01:35:13.174 --> 01:35:13.774
Interesting.

01:35:14.123 --> 01:35:19.046
So potentially the collision was much more complex than it initially seemed, and there

01:35:19.066 --> 01:35:22.788
may not be missing land, but new land we weren't aware of.

01:35:24.889 --> 01:35:29.831
So one way or another, 40 to 50 million years ago, we are getting Himalayas as we know them.

01:35:30.191 --> 01:35:35.394
To put that into context for people, that is a little bit younger than the Rocky Mountains

01:35:36.286 --> 01:35:41.948
here in North America, which I believe are thought to have begun forming in earnest around

01:35:42.229 --> 01:35:43.589
70 million years ago.

01:35:44.309 --> 01:35:47.591
And at that time, the Appalachian Mountains were already ancient.

01:35:47.811 --> 01:35:48.051
Yes.

01:35:49.552 --> 01:35:54.354
I also saw a post that made the point that the fact that the Himalayas started being

01:35:54.714 --> 01:36:00.777
up-thrust about 50 million years ago also brings into perspective how quickly these

01:36:00.827 --> 01:36:05.068
mountains grew, because they became the highest mountain range in the world in that amount

01:36:05.089 --> 01:36:05.309
of time.

01:36:05.529 --> 01:36:05.689
Yes.

01:36:06.029 --> 01:36:10.411
And in fairness to the Appalachians, they had been eroding for a while.

01:36:13.352 --> 01:36:16.534
It's the mountain equivalent of bald spots now.

01:36:16.994 --> 01:36:20.135
These are mountains past their prime.

01:36:20.744 --> 01:36:26.526
There's a ton more that we could discuss about the Himalayas in terms of what lives there

01:36:26.586 --> 01:36:27.107
and all that.

01:36:27.687 --> 01:36:35.230
What always strikes me is that the Himalayas, my favorite geological note about the Himalayas

01:36:35.350 --> 01:36:41.433
is that they are so large and so dramatic in their formation that they affected the

01:36:41.593 --> 01:36:42.113
climate.

01:36:42.985 --> 01:36:48.847
Not like obviously the local climate, like the modern monsoon cycle as we know it in

01:36:48.887 --> 01:36:55.871
that area is influenced by the existence of the Himalayas, but it has also been suggested,

01:36:56.351 --> 01:37:03.654
though not necessarily a definite by itself thing, that the rising of the Himalayas is

01:37:03.747 --> 01:37:08.469
related to the cooling trend across the Cenozoic era.

01:37:08.929 --> 01:37:14.211
Because the Himalayas are a whole lot of rock put up into the atmosphere, which makes it

01:37:14.251 --> 01:37:18.292
a prime area for chemical weathering, which absorbs carbon dioxide.

01:37:18.312 --> 01:37:22.614
And when you draw a carbon dioxide out of the atmosphere, you create cooling temperatures.

01:37:23.014 --> 01:37:23.374
Yup.

01:37:23.544 --> 01:37:30.448
The rising of the Himalayas might have been a factor in the cooling trend that led to

01:37:30.488 --> 01:37:34.670
the formation of grasslands and the eventual onset of the ice age.

01:37:35.230 --> 01:37:41.534
It's like the climactic equivalent of cooling rods being put into a uranium reactor.

01:37:41.594 --> 01:37:45.756
We thrust these mountains up into the sky and just sucked all the carbon out.

01:37:45.966 --> 01:37:49.447
So there's a whole bunch of cool Himalaya stuff, which I suppose must be, you know,

01:37:49.547 --> 01:37:51.128
for a Himalayas episode at some point.

01:37:51.268 --> 01:37:55.490
If anyone asks for it, yeah, you know, maybe, maybe somebody will want that.

01:37:56.270 --> 01:37:58.872
But this is going to kind of bring us to the end of our discussion.

01:37:58.972 --> 01:38:01.953
Of course, like you said, the Himalayas could be their whole episode.

