01:38:35.364 --> 01:38:40.026
Today's patron question comes from Ian, who writes, "Not sure if you've answered this

01:38:40.086 --> 01:38:46.309
before, but I'm curious how primates came to live in both South America and Africa if

01:38:46.349 --> 01:38:49.510
those continents split before primates evolved.

01:38:50.051 --> 01:38:51.371
Is my timeline just off?

01:38:51.771 --> 01:38:55.433
Did one group sail across the ocean and then become New World monkeys?"

01:38:56.153 --> 01:38:56.954
Good question, Ian.

01:38:57.254 --> 01:38:57.534
Yes.

01:38:57.794 --> 01:38:59.835
So you got it right off the bat.

01:38:59.982 --> 01:39:02.464
One group sailed across the ocean and became New World monkeys.

01:39:02.724 --> 01:39:08.567
We did actually talk about this in an earlier episode, although in Ian's defense, that was

01:39:08.827 --> 01:39:11.488
four years ago in episode seven.

01:39:11.849 --> 01:39:12.809
That's so crazy.

01:39:13.449 --> 01:39:19.492
So yes, you're absolutely right that old world New World monkeys, African, South American,

01:39:20.033 --> 01:39:22.874
evolved after those continents were no longer hanging out.

01:39:23.054 --> 01:39:23.875
Yeah.

01:39:23.903 --> 01:39:28.805
And the old world today includes a lot of those monkeys and also apes, lemurs, et cetera.

01:39:28.985 --> 01:39:31.607
But yeah, there's a bunch of South American, Central American monkeys.

01:39:32.187 --> 01:39:37.710
And as far as the fossil record tells us, yeah, the suggestion is there's multiple options.

01:39:37.930 --> 01:39:41.672
It could be that they rafted across from Africa to South America.

01:39:42.052 --> 01:39:48.215
Which, as we've discussed, sounds insane to suggest, but is a thing we know happens.

01:39:48.245 --> 01:39:49.045
Yes.

01:39:49.085 --> 01:39:56.388
So a chunk of landmass or a chunk of jungle gets washed out to sea during a storm or something

01:39:56.548 --> 01:40:02.971
and can travel on ocean currents and eventually drop off whatever animals have hitched a ride.

01:40:03.411 --> 01:40:09.053
But there's also suggestion for some sort of land bridge of islands that could be more

01:40:09.166 --> 01:40:14.048
hopped across than just a full three hour tour ocean voyage.

01:40:14.888 --> 01:40:23.112
That if there were periodic land, if not full bridge connection, but smatterings of islands

01:40:23.152 --> 01:40:26.013
or something that could have aided in that passage.

01:40:26.613 --> 01:40:30.375
And there's a lot of researchers who seem to like that idea.

01:40:31.185 --> 01:40:35.427
So it may not have been just fully sailing, but little shorter trips.

01:40:36.067 --> 01:40:40.989
Because Africa and South America, and indeed like the whole of the Atlantic Ocean was open

01:40:41.049 --> 01:40:44.311
well before we see these monkeys show up in the New World.

01:40:44.811 --> 01:40:50.494
Incidentally, the same trip that the monkeys make seems to have been how rodents got to

01:40:50.534 --> 01:40:51.094
South America.

01:40:51.494 --> 01:40:52.635
Rodents did the same thing.

01:40:52.655 --> 01:40:53.976
So we have New World rodents.

01:40:54.543 --> 01:40:57.625
Now, I did see one thing that mentioned that the only other option is that they would have

01:40:57.645 --> 01:41:01.026
had to go up and around into North America and then come down.

01:41:01.087 --> 01:41:01.247
Right.

01:41:01.567 --> 01:41:08.971
Up into the Middle East, across Asia, all the way up north into North America, all the

01:41:09.011 --> 01:41:14.174
way down North America and into South America, which there is one group of primates that

01:41:14.194 --> 01:41:14.654
did that.

01:41:15.395 --> 01:41:17.196
And it was us, it was Homo sapiens.

01:41:17.376 --> 01:41:18.657
But way later.

01:41:18.803 --> 01:41:20.464
We're here to kill everything.

01:41:20.484 --> 01:41:21.424
We came a long way.

01:41:21.444 --> 01:41:23.645
We finally found these monkeys.

01:41:24.506 --> 01:41:29.708
So like that could have happened, but there's zero fossil evidence that that is the path

01:41:29.748 --> 01:41:30.049
they took.

01:41:30.069 --> 01:41:30.249
Right.

01:41:30.289 --> 01:41:35.371
The fossils exist in Africa and Southern Asia and South America.

01:41:35.531 --> 01:41:40.194
So it's more likely that they made their way across the water.

01:41:40.354 --> 01:41:41.594
They went on an incredible journey.

01:41:41.755 --> 01:41:41.975
Yes.

01:41:42.202 --> 01:41:42.702
Like India.

01:41:44.884 --> 01:41:50.266
So yeah, I love, when I was reading the question, is this maybe what happened?

01:41:50.346 --> 01:41:50.547
Yep.

01:41:50.587 --> 01:41:50.787
Nope.

